---
// About: what Inferview is and how it is built.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TRACKS, MODULE_COUNT } from '../data/tracks.ts';
import { siteCounts, plural } from '../lib/content.ts';
import { firebaseConfigured } from '../lib/firebase.ts';

const counts = await siteCounts();
---
<Base title="About" description="What Inferview is and how it is built." active="none">
  <div class="wrap page narrow prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>Inferview is free interview preparation for AI engineering, written for engineers who already ship software. It is {TRACKS.length} tracks and {MODULE_COUNT} modules in one order, with {plural(counts.lessons, 'lesson')}, {plural(counts.problems, 'problem')} you can answer in the browser{counts.labs > 0 && `, and ${plural(counts.labs, 'lab')}`}.</p>
    <p>The reading material for this field already exists and much of it is good. Inferview tries to add the practice: problems that grade you, a daily problem, and review cards that come back on a schedule. Lessons link out to the original sources rather than restating them.</p>
    <p>Every number on a page is measured, taken from a published source, or worked out in front of you. Scenarios are labelled as scenarios. A company name appears on a problem only with a public source that names the company and the topic.</p>
    <p>The site is static. Progress lives in your browser{firebaseConfigured && ' and, if you sign in with Google, in your own Firestore document'}; the <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> lists exactly what that means. Code is MIT licensed and content is CC BY-SA 4.0.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/learn">Learn</a> page lists every track, module and lesson in order.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
